{:receipt :kotobase/health-without-a-hydrate
 :date "2026-08-17"
 :worker "kotobase-protocols-worker"
 :version "3c9b7376-3205-4011-9618-0accaa0b2e19"
 :merge "eb57e38095e"

 :how-this-was-found
 "By asking what produced a number I had already measured more than twenty
  times in one day and never questioned. Every latency figure in today's
  receipts came from GET /health, and I had described it throughout as `the
  hydrate half of a served request` without once reading the handler."

 :the-defect
 "`kotobase.protocols.router/handle` answers /health from four constants --
  :ok, :service, :surface, :apex -- and touches no store. The shell's hydrate
  is unconditional and sits ABOVE the router, so every request rebuilt the
  whole graph and then called a branch that reads none of it.

  `handle-request` already short-circuits three paths before the hydrate:
  /capabilities, /_diag/query-memo and /_diag/health. /health was simply not
  among them. That is why /_diag/health answered in 0.1-0.24s all day while
  /health took seconds -- the two differ by one line in a cond chain, not by
  what they compute."

 :cost-per-call
 {:cpu-ms 1364 :r2-class-b-gets 85 :bytes 11400000
  :returns "four constant fields"
  :note "CPU and GET counts from the instrumented harness replaying this
         graph's real blocks (2026-08-17-fold-cost-at-production-shape.edn),
         not from workerd."}

 :why-it-is-the-worst-possible-route-for-it
 "Health is the most polled route a service has -- uptime checks, load
  balancers, monitoring. Six hosts polled once a minute is ~259,200 calls a
  month: 353M CPU-ms and 22M Class B operations, about $15/month at the rates
  captured today, to return a constant. My own probing added several thousand
  of those calls."

 :measured
 {:method "10 sequential GETs per host over one connection, immediately before
           and after the deploy"
  :before-ms {:sparql 6231 :gremlin 4827 :graphql 3935}
  :after-p50-ms {:sparql 18 :gremlin 15 :graphql 15 :s3 12 :atproto 14 :pinning 14}
  :factor "~300x"
  :body-unchanged "{:ok true, :service :kotobase.protocols/sparql, :surface :sparql, :apex \"kotobase.net\"} -- byte for byte"}

 :the-guard-that-makes-it-a-short-circuit
 "`static-health` reproduces the router's own mounted-surface check and returns
  nil when the router would NOT have taken its health branch, so an unmounted
  surface falls through to exactly the old path. Assuming every host is mounted
  would have turned a short-circuit into a behaviour change: the split
  single-surface shells (net-kotobase/cypher) mount a subset via select-keys.
  Dropping that guard turns exactly the unmounted-surface assertion red and
  nothing else -- demonstrated, not asserted.
  103 tests / 700 assertions, 0 failures."

 :consequence-for-the-other-receipts
 "/health is no longer usable as an external probe for hydrate cost, and that
  is what every 2,800 ms and 4,400 ms figure in today's receipts was measuring.
  Those numbers stand -- they measured the hydrate, correctly, and the
  read-path fix that moved 4,400 -> 2,800 is unaffected -- but the probe point
  is gone. Measuring hydrate from outside now needs an authenticated query or
  an instrumented /_diag route. The fold-cost receipt is unaffected: it
  measures CPU and R2 operations in an instrumented harness over this graph's
  real blocks, not over HTTP."

 :fifth-of-its-kind-today
 "Capability present, one wire missing. GraphSync: seven namespaces
  implementing /ipfs/graphsync/2.0.0, required by no deployed worker. The
  datalake: catalog, columnar, Parquet/Arrow readers and the prefetch
  trampoline all landed, lake.kotobase.net still NXDOMAIN. Materialised views:
  the whole projection library pinned and compiled into the bundle, zero views
  declared on the production graph. `:query-statistics`: implemented, unused
  for eleven days, and wiring it took IC08 from 11.4x to 1.85x. And this one --
  a cond chain missing one line.

  None of these needed new capability. All of them needed someone to connect
  what was already built. That is worth stating as a property of this
  workspace rather than as five separate incidents."}
